  "title": "# Build: handoff-2 (FABLE, high thinking: replaces the cross-session coordination protocol = founda…",
  "asked": "# Build: handoff-2 (FABLE, high thinking: replaces the cross-session coordination protocol = foundation) ## What Brett said (verbatim, Nov 4 2026, voice) \"Is there a way to have two sessions working on the project at the same time without building stuff that's gonna conflict with the other one?\" ... \"Doesn't it already exist in the handoff?\" (Amber: THREADS/handoff notes are honor system; nothing enforces it.) \"Let's scratch the whole handoff system as we know it and create one based on spec. Here's what I want to do. I want to press the button in the console. It'll say prepare handoff to share a second account. And then it will open the browser tab in Claude, or then it'll give me a cut and paste prompt that I'll prompt into the new session from the other account, and run it. And then they'll both be working side by side and being able to talk to each other.\" Book to a free named agent. ## Do 1. **Spec first** (`framework/spec/HANDOFF.md`, `handoff@1` / `session-link@1`): how two Claude sessions (same or different Claude accounts, claude.ai web or Claude Code) pair up, talk, split the work and avoid conflicts. Retire the old THREADS/handoff-notes system: migrate anything useful, then mark it deprecated and remove it once the new one is live. 2. **Console button: 'Prepare handoff to a second account'** (Home card, Build manager, banner chip, and as a voice/MCP command). It: - snapshots the current state: what this session is doing, open builds/PRs, decisions, rules, which areas each side owns, and what to pick up; - generates a **one-time pairing token** (short expiry, owner-approved, revocable) on axHub; - shows a **copy button with a ready-to-paste prompt** (plus an 'Open Claude' button that opens claude.ai/new in a new tab). The prompt tells the new session how to connect to axHub with the token, load the snapshot, claim its areas and say hi to the first session. - NOTE for Brett in the UI: the second account needs the axHub connector added once (show the exact steps). 3. **Talking side by side**: a session channel on axHub (MCP tools `session.send`, `session.inbox`, `session.claim`, `session.release`, `session.status`). Messages show live in a **Sessions** panel in the console (like a group chat: you can read along and post too), reuse the bugs-board group-chat style if it has landed. Each session sees who's online and what they're on. 4. **No conflicts, enforced**: area/file **claims** (leases with heartbeat and expiry). A session must claim an area (Terminal, banner, kernel, a path glob) before editing it. The other side gets 'claimed by Session B' and either waits, asks over the channel, or takes another task. The build queue uses the same claims (a `touches` field on builds; never run two builds on overlapping claims). Pre-commit/CI check: fail if a commit touches files outside the session's claims. 5. **Hand back / end**: 'End pairing' releases claims, writes a closing summary to both and revokes the token. 6. Audit log of everything both sessions do, visible in Requests ledger / Codebase changes. 7. Safety: tokens are scoped to the repo/project, approval is owner-only, the second session can't change axHub policy or approvals, and every held call still goes through Vinnie. 8. Tests (two simulated sessions racing for the same area), docs, UI Review shots (non-blocking), PR, auto-land, redeploy the console and axHub. Report in _claude/builds/reports/. Commits clean. ## ADDENDUM 1 (Brett, voice, Nov 4): REPLACES the copy-paste prompt in step 2 \"Actually, don't give me a prompt to hand off. Just point me to a file like 'read such and such file'. Actually, no. Make it so that when I type /sync ses or /sync amberx, it will automatically write a file on the repo that each project can read and jump in.\" - **`/sync` slash command** (Claude Code custom command in `.claude/commands/sync.md`, synced to every project via claude-sync; args: `amberx`, `ses`/`session`, or a project name). Typing it **writes/refreshes a sync file in…",
